description:
**Test gap**: The pkix-path-builder integration tests (pkix-path-builder/tests/integration.rs) test empty pool, wrong pool, and correct two-cert chain. There is no test confirming that build_path correctly handles a pool containing a self-signed certificate that is NOT the trust anchor.

**Why it matters**: The cycle guard for self-signed non-anchor certs depends on subject-name matching (lib.rs:181-186). A test would confirm this guard actually prevents unbounded recursion and returns NoPathFound rather than hanging or panicking.

**Proposed test**:

```rust
/// A self-signed cert in the pool that is not the trust anchor must
/// not cause an infinite loop — the cycle guard must detect it.
#[test]
fn test_build_path_self_signed_non_anchor_in_pool() {
    let ee = pkits_cert("ValidCertificatePathTest1EE"); // issuer = GoodCA
    let anchor = pkits_trust_anchor(); // TrustAnchorRootCertificate (subject = root)

    // A self-signed cert (subject == issuer) that is unrelated to the chain.
    // TrustAnchorRootCertificate is self-signed but IS the anchor; use a different self-signed cert.
    // Since we don't have a spare self-signed cert in the fixture set, use the anchor cert
    // *not* registered as an anchor — it IS self-signed and a CA.
    let ta_cert = pkits_cert("TrustAnchorRootCertificate");
    let mut pool = CertPool::new();
    pool.add(ta_cert); // self-signed but not registered as anchor

    // build_path should find no path because GoodCACert is not in the pool.
    let err = build_path(&ee, &pool, &[anchor])
        .expect_err("build_path must fail when only a self-signed non-anchor is in pool");
    assert!(matches!(err, pkix_path_builder::Error::NoPathFound));
}
```

**Location**: pkix-path-builder/tests/integration.rs (currently 122 lines, add after line 122)
